  <h1>Types of Workplace Harassment</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Riley Chen (RC), Employment &amp; Civil Rights Practice Lead. Updated May 2026.</em></p>
  <h2>Quid pro quo sexual harassment</h2><p>A supervisor conditions an employment benefit (promotion, raise, continued employment) on submission to sexual advances. A single incident is sufficient. The employer is strictly liable — no affirmative defense available.</p>
  <h2>Hostile environment sexual harassment</h2><p>Unwelcome sexual conduct severe or pervasive enough to create an abusive work environment. Includes physical touching, sexual comments, sexually explicit materials, and repeated propositions. Employer liability depends on whether a supervisor or co-worker was the harasser.</p>
  <h2>Racial and national origin harassment</h2><p>Racial slurs, derogatory jokes, racially hostile conduct. One severe slur from a supervisor may suffice; repeated lesser conduct establishes pervasiveness. Pattern evidence across multiple victims strengthens the claim.</p>
  <h2>Disability harassment</h2><p>Mocking, excluding, or creating a hostile environment based on disability status. The ADA prohibits harassment based on actual disability, record of disability, or being regarded as having a disability. Same severe-or-pervasive standard as Title VII.</p>
  <h2>Age harassment (ADEA)</h2><p>Systematic demeaning treatment of workers 40+ based on age. Isolated age jokes rarely meet the threshold; a sustained campaign of age-based ridicule or exclusion can. Unlike Title VII, ADEA does not have compensatory/punitive damage caps in the same form &mdash; liquidated damages for willful violations double back pay instead.</p>
